# Exported symbols of libgoldberry -- the complete native surface.
#
# Everything is compiled with hidden visibility and the statically linked
# upstreams are excluded from re-export, so a symbol reaches Java only by being
# listed here. The build turns this file into a linker version script on Linux,
# an -exported_symbols_list on macOS, and a .def on Windows, and forces each
# symbol to be pulled out of its static archive with -u.
#
# This file is the mirror of the Java binding registry: a symbol bound in Java
# but missing here fails at load time with an unresolved-symbol error, and a
# symbol here that nothing binds is dead weight. Keeping the two in step is the
# same discipline ADR-0010 applies to struct layouts.
#
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. One symbol per line.
#
# ASCII ONLY, including in comments. CMake's file(STRINGS) extracts runs of
# printable ASCII, so a non-ASCII byte splits the line -- a comment containing
# one returns its tail as a separate element that no longer starts with '#', and
# the parser hands that fragment to the linker as a symbol name. The build now
# rejects anything that is not a valid C identifier, but keeping this file ASCII
# avoids the problem at its source. (Hence "sec. 4" rather than the section sign
# used elsewhere in the docs.)

# --- libgoldberry's own surface ---------------------------------------------
goldberry_abi_version
goldberry_layout_table
goldberry_layout_count

# What this build of the platform layer can actually do, whatever its API says
# (sec. G32, ADR-0325). On Linux three of SDL's desktop integrations -- the system
# theme, the portal file dialog and the input method on X11 -- are compiled out
# when a -dev package was absent on the build machine, and the calls then answer
# "the desktop does not say" rather than failing. One word, read back at start-up,
# is what tells those two apart.
goldberry_platform_capabilities

# Calls a YGMeasureFunc upcall stub and reports what the struct-by-value return
# delivered. See sec. 3.1 and ADR-0017.
goldberry_probe_measure

# Tells GTK which window system to use, so the two halves of one process agree.
# SDL is asked for X11 first on Linux and GDK prefers Wayland when asked nothing,
# which on an XWayland desktop makes the application's window an X11 window and
# its GTK surfaces Wayland surfaces -- invisible until `web-view` tries to
# reparent one into the other (ADR-0442).
goldberry_prefer_gtk_backend

# --- SDL3 (sec. 4, backend SPI) ----------------------------------------------
# Lifecycle, error and version. Enough to prove SDL3 is reachable through this
# list and to report which SDL is linked in; windowing follows.
SDL_Init
SDL_InitSubSystem
SDL_QuitSubSystem
SDL_WasInit
SDL_Quit
SDL_GetError
SDL_ClearError
SDL_GetVersion
SDL_GetRevision
SDL_GetCurrentVideoDriver

# Where SDL's own log messages go (ADR-0443).
#
# SDL writes to stderr -- a driver it could not load, a hint it did not
# understand -- which on an application that configured logging is a line from
# nowhere, with no level and no name, in a console nothing is capturing. One
# symbol redirects the lot into SLF4J.
#
# The obvious neighbour is deliberately absent. SDL_SetLogPriorities would lower
# SDL's own per-category thresholds so that more of it reached the bridge, and
# that is a decision about verbosity this toolkit has no business making for
# every application that links it. The destination is ours to fix; what is worth
# saying stays SDL's.
SDL_SetLogOutputFunction

# The desktop's light-or-dark setting, and the one call that answers it on all
# three platforms -- the alternatives are AppleInterfaceStyle, the
# AppsUseLightTheme registry value and the XDG settings portal (sec. G26,
# ADR-0322). The change arrives as SDL_EVENT_SYSTEM_THEME_CHANGED, which needs no
# symbol of its own: it comes through the event queue that is already pumped.
SDL_GetSystemTheme
SDL_SetHint

# A URL handed to the desktop's own handler for its scheme -- the browser, the
# mail client. What sec. 2's `link` opens an `href` through (ADR-0346).
SDL_OpenURL

# The native window handle, which ARCHITECTURE.md sec. 12 has promised since day
# one and nothing had built: "backends expose raw native window handles for apps
# embedding external renderers". Its first consumer is `web-view`, which cannot
# be a widget without one -- a page is a platform window, and putting it inside
# this one means reparenting it into this one's X11 Window, HWND or NSWindow
# (ADR-0441, ADR-0442).
#
# The property NAMES are string #defines in SDL's headers rather than symbols,
# so only the three getters cross. SDL_GetWindowProperties returns an id;
# SDL_GetNumberProperty reads the X11 Window, which is an integer; and
# SDL_GetPointerProperty reads the HWND and the NSWindow, which are not.
SDL_GetWindowProperties
SDL_GetNumberProperty
SDL_GetPointerProperty

# Windowing and the CPU present path (sec. 4, sec. 5).
SDL_CreateWindow
SDL_DestroyWindow
SDL_ShowWindow
SDL_SetWindowTitle
SDL_MaximizeWindow
SDL_RestoreWindow
SDL_GetWindowSize
SDL_GetWindowSizeInPixels

# The floor a user may drag a window down to (ADR-0304). The window manager
# enforces it, which is the whole reason this is a platform call rather than a
# clamp in the resize handler: clamping after the fact means the frame is
# already the wrong size and the user is already fighting the pointer.
SDL_SetWindowMinimumSize
SDL_GetWindowDisplayScale
SDL_GetDisplayForWindow
SDL_GetCurrentDisplayMode
SDL_GetWindowID
SDL_GetWindowSurface
SDL_UpdateWindowSurfaceRects
SDL_DestroyWindowSurface

# Popup windows (sec. 4, sec. 7). A popup is a real platform window parented to
# another and positioned in its coordinates, which is what lets a menu, a
# tooltip or a select's list escape the bounds of the window that opened it --
# the thing the in-window overlay layer (ADR-0100) deliberately cannot do.
# Position and size are here because every consumer needs them: a menu near a
# screen edge flips, and an autocomplete's list narrows as it filters.
SDL_CreatePopupWindow
SDL_SetWindowPosition
SDL_SetWindowSize

# Where a popup is allowed to be (sec. 7's "placement with flip/shift when near
# edges"). The usable bounds exclude a taskbar or a dock, which is the whole
# reason it is not the display's full size; the window's own position is what
# turns a popup's owner-relative placement into the screen coordinates the work
# area is in.
SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds
SDL_GetWindowPosition

# The modifier keys, read at the moment a pointer event is translated rather
# than latched from the last key event. A window that loses focus while Shift is
# held never sees the key release, and a latched flag would stay stuck down.
# sec. 3 asks a knob for a "modifier for fine adjustment"; sec. 2.3's Ctrl+click
# and Shift+click want the same thing. See ADR-0089.
SDL_GetModState

# Where the pointer is on the desktop, right now.
#
# Polled at the moment a pointer event is translated, like SDL_GetModState above
# and for a sharper reason: on macOS a popup window's mouse events arrive with
# coordinates in the OWNER window's space, and stale ones at that -- SDL only
# rewrites them into the target window's space when the event's NSWindow is not
# the key window, and a mouse-UP is delivered to the key window. The window a
# popup's release is attributed to and the space its coordinates are in
# therefore disagree, which is a click that lands nowhere (ADR-0211).
#
# A global position plus the owner's own position is the one reading that does
# not depend on which window the platform thinks the event belongs to.
SDL_GetGlobalMouseState

# Event pumping, and SDL_PushEvent for the cross-thread wakeup.
#
# SDL_PumpEvents is deliberately absent: SDL_PollEvent and SDL_WaitEventTimeout
# pump for themselves, and nothing here ever wanted the queue refreshed without
# reading it. It was exported and force-linked for a year and bound by nothing,
# which is the dead weight the rule at the top of this file names.
SDL_PollEvent
SDL_WaitEventTimeout
SDL_PushEvent

# The event watch (ADR-0060). Windows and macOS run a modal loop while a window
# is being resized and do not return from event pumping until the drag ends; a
# watch is called from inside that pump, and is the only place a frame can be
# drawn while it is running.
SDL_AddEventWatch
SDL_RemoveEventWatch

# Cursors (sec. 7.3). Process-global in SDL: SDL_SetCursor changes what the mouse
# looks like, and the pointer is over one window at a time.
SDL_CreateSystemCursor
SDL_SetCursor
SDL_DestroyCursor
SDL_ShowCursor
SDL_HideCursor

# Text input (sec. 7.1). SDL3 does not deliver SDL_EVENT_TEXT_INPUT until a
# window asks for it: text input is off at startup and per-window, because
# turning it on may raise an on-screen keyboard or an IME candidate window.
# Nothing bound these until `text-input` existed to want them, so the committed
# text the event loop already knew how to read never arrived on SDL3.
SDL_StartTextInput
SDL_StopTextInput
SDL_TextInputActive

# Where the text being typed is, so an input method can put its candidate window
# beside it (docs/gaps.md G15). Without this the list opens wherever the
# compositor guesses, which on a large window is routinely over the text being
# composed. Logical coordinates, like SDL's own units for the call.
SDL_SetTextInputArea

# The clipboard (sec. 4). SDL_GetClipboardText allocates and the caller frees,
# which is the only reason SDL_free is on this list -- it is SDL's allocator and
# not the C library's, and freeing across that boundary is undefined.
SDL_GetClipboardText
SDL_SetClipboardText
SDL_HasClipboardText
SDL_free
#
# And the clipboard's other half, which is bytes under a MIME type: an image
# pasted from a browser, a document's own format copied between two of its
# windows (sec. 4, ADR-0286).
#
# `SDL_SetClipboardData` is **lazy** and this is the whole of why it needs three
# symbols rather than one: an application does not put bytes on a clipboard, it
# advertises the types it can produce and is asked to produce one when somebody
# pastes. So a write hands over two callbacks and a list of MIME types, and the
# bytes stay ours -- in an arena the cleanup callback closes when the offer is
# replaced. That is the second upcall family in this library after Yoga's measure
# (ADR-0017), and the first where the *caller* owns memory across it.
SDL_SetClipboardData
SDL_ClearClipboardData
#
# The read is the ordinary shape: SDL allocates, we copy, SDL_free above closes
# the loop. `SDL_HasClipboardData` is the cheap question -- on X11 and Wayland a
# read is a round trip to the owning client and `has` is answered from what the
# compositor already said.
SDL_GetClipboardData
SDL_HasClipboardData
#
# And what the clipboard is actually offering, which is the question a paste that
# found nothing has to be able to answer: "no image" and "no image *this* toolkit
# can decode" look identical from `SDL_HasClipboardData` alone, and the difference
# is the whole of what a user needs to be told. One SDL_free releases the array
# and its strings together.
SDL_GetClipboardMimeTypes

# The system tray (sec. 9's `tray-icon`). SDL owns the platform menu here --
# these entries are drawn by the desktop shell and not by Goldberry, which is
# why a tray menu is a *description* handed over rather than a widget tree.
# SDL_UpdateTrays is deliberately absent: SDL calls it from its own event loop,
# and this toolkit pumps events.
#
# SDL_GetTrayEntryChecked is the one read-back: SDL toggles a checkbox itself
# before it calls back, so it is the only way to learn what the user chose.
SDL_CreateTray
SDL_DestroyTray
SDL_SetTrayIcon
SDL_SetTrayTooltip
SDL_CreateTrayMenu
SDL_CreateTraySubmenu
SDL_InsertTrayEntryAt
SDL_SetTrayEntryCallback
SDL_GetTrayEntryChecked

# A surface over pixels of ours, which the tray icon is: SDL wants an
# SDL_Surface and Goldberry has a painted BGRA buffer. Nothing is copied -- the
# surface is a view, so the buffer must outlive it, which is the same rule
# BlendImage states for the frame path.
SDL_CreateSurfaceFrom
SDL_DestroySurface

# The window's own icon -- the taskbar's, the dock's, the switcher's
# (docs/gaps.md G40, ADR-0351). One surface for the 100% size and the rest
# hung off it as alternates, which is how SDL picks one per display scale.
SDL_SetWindowIcon
SDL_AddSurfaceAlternateImage

# The platform's own file dialogs (docs/gaps.md G9). Export a board as a PNG,
# import an image, open a snapshot: all three are a path the user chose, and a
# toolkit that drew its own file browser would be drawing the one part of the
# desktop the desktop is certain to have already. Asynchronous -- the answer
# arrives in an upcall, which on Linux comes back from the DBus thread that
# talks to the XDG portal.
SDL_ShowOpenFileDialog
SDL_ShowSaveFileDialog
SDL_ShowOpenFolderDialog

# --- Blend2D (sec. 5, paint & raster) ----------------------------------------
# The image and context surface: enough to rasterize a frame into the buffer the
# platform lends us. Paths, gradients, strokes and text follow. See ADR-0031.
#
# Note the snake_case: this revision of Blend2D renamed its C API from
# blImageInit to bl_image_init. A symbol list copied from an older example fails
# to link, which is one more reason the ref is a commit SHA (ADR-0030).

# Which Blend2D is linked in.
bl_runtime_query_info

# Images. The external-data constructor is what the frame path uses: Goldberry
# never asks Blend2D to allocate pixels it will paint into, it hands over the
# ones it already has.
bl_image_init_as_from_data
bl_image_destroy
bl_image_get_data
#
# Decoding, which is the one place that rule has to bend (sec. 5, ADR-0283).
# Only the decoder knows how big a PNG is, so it must allocate: an empty image is
# initialised, handed the encoded bytes, and its pixels are copied into a buffer
# Java owns before the handle is destroyed on the same call. Nothing keeps a
# Blend2D allocation past `Image.decode`.
#
# `bl_image_read_from_data` takes a codec array, and NULL means the built-in
# ones -- PNG, JPEG and QOI are compiled into this library already
# (CMakeLists.txt's Blend2D options), so no codec object crosses and none of the
# `bl_image_codec_*` family is bound. There is no encode symbol here for the
# same reason a dash is not a symbol (ADR-0278): PNG is written by
# `image.png.PngEncoder`, in java.base, over Deflater.
bl_image_init
bl_image_read_from_data
#
# And the conversion after it, because a PNG without an alpha channel decodes to
# XRGB32 while every buffer in the toolkit is premultiplied BGRA. Converting
# once at decode is what keeps `Frame.drawImage` from having to ask what format
# it is blitting.
bl_image_convert
#
# And a resampled COPY, which is a different operation from a scaled blit and
# the one image operation this library could do and did not (ADR-0428).
# bl_context_blit_scaled_image_d below resamples on its way to a surface and
# keeps nothing; this produces pixels that outlive the call -- a thumbnail
# written to disk, an icon scaled once and drawn a hundred times.
#
# It allocates, which makes it the second exception to "Goldberry never asks
# Blend2D for pixels" after the decoder, and it is held to the same discipline:
# the destination is destroyed on the call that made it, once its rows have been
# copied into a buffer Java owns.
#
# The filter argument is mandatory in C, so the choice exists whether or not a
# caller is offered it. BLImageScaleFilter is on the layout table.
bl_image_scale

# Rendering context lifecycle.
bl_context_init_as
bl_context_end
bl_context_destroy

# State: the display scale reaches the rasterizer as a transform.
bl_context_apply_transform_op
bl_context_set_comp_op

# Which points a path encloses (ADR-0427). Blend2D fills non-zero by default, so
# a sub-path wound the other way inside an outer one is still filled and a hole
# cannot be cut. Even-odd counts crossings instead, and a point inside two
# sub-paths is crossed twice and left alone -- which is what knocks a box's own
# rectangle out of its drop shadow, the deviation from CSS that ADR-0310 wrote
# down and could not fix.
#
# The obvious alternative is not on this list because it is not in the library.
# Blend2D clips to a RECTANGLE and to nothing else: `bl_context_clip_to_rect_d`
# and `_i` above are the whole of its clipping surface, and there is no
# `clip_to_path` in `core/context.h` to export. A fill rule is not the cheaper of
# two ways to cut a hole here; it is the only one.
bl_context_set_fill_rule

# Drawing.
#
# Not bl_context_clear_all and not bl_context_flush: a frame starts with a
# fill_all over the whole clip box, and bl_context_end is what flushes. Both were
# bound in Java, called by nothing, and exported for the binding that called
# them.
bl_context_fill_all_rgba32
bl_context_fill_rect_d_rgba32

# Fonts, and the glyphs HarfBuzz chose (sec. 6, ADR-0034). Bytes -> data ->
# face -> font, three objects for the same reason HarfBuzz needs three: the
# file, the typeface, and the size it is used at are different things. Only the
# from-data constructors are bound; Goldberry never gives Blend2D a file path.
bl_font_data_init
bl_font_data_create_from_data
bl_font_data_destroy
bl_font_face_init
bl_font_face_create_from_data
bl_font_face_destroy
bl_font_init
bl_font_create_from_face
bl_font_destroy

# Where the baseline goes. Scaled by the font's size, unlike the design metrics
# on the face, which is what makes them the ones a paint pass wants.
bl_font_get_metrics

# The call the whole font chain exists for.
bl_context_fill_glyph_run_d_rgba32

# Paths, and the icons drawn with them (sec. 6.3, ADR-0043). Lucide's 1544 icons
# are SVG path data in a 24x24 box, so what is bound is the set of path commands
# SVG has and no more: the curve constructors, the elliptic arc that `A` maps
# onto directly, and the two "smooth" forms that `S` and `T` map onto. Binding
# fewer would mean reimplementing them in Java against Blend2D's own conventions
# for the reflected control point, which is exactly the arithmetic a rasterizer
# should be trusted with.
bl_path_init
bl_path_destroy
bl_path_reset
bl_path_get_size
bl_path_move_to
bl_path_line_to
bl_path_quad_to
bl_path_cubic_to
bl_path_smooth_quad_to
bl_path_smooth_cubic_to
bl_path_elliptic_arc_to
bl_path_close

# An icon is stroked, not filled: Lucide draws 2px round strokes rather than
# filled shapes, so the stroke options are as much a part of the icon path as
# the geometry is. Fill is bound too, for the shapes that are filled.
bl_context_set_stroke_width
bl_context_set_stroke_caps
bl_context_set_stroke_join
bl_context_fill_path_d_rgba32
bl_context_stroke_path_d_rgba32

# The miter limit (sec. 5, ADR-0278). One double, and it closes a gap the enums
# admit to in their own javadoc: BlendStrokeJoin binds three of Blend2D's five,
# and the two omitted are the miter-with-a-fallback variants, "whose behaviour
# depends on the miter limit -- and nothing binds that yet". Something binds it
# now.
#
# Its three obvious neighbours are deliberately absent.
# `bl_context_set_stroke_dash_array`, `_dash_offset` and the `bl_array_*` needed
# to carry a pattern were added here and taken back out: Blend2D stores a dash
# array in BLStrokeOptions, retains it, copies it and saves it across
# save/restore -- and never strokes with it. `core/pathstroke.cpp` is 988 lines
# and the word "dash" does not appear in it. The calls return BL_SUCCESS and the
# line comes out solid, which is the worst way for a gap to present itself.
#
# So dashing is Goldberry's own, over `paint.Path`, and this list stays five
# symbols shorter than the first draft of ADR-0278 assumed.
bl_context_set_stroke_miter_limit

# Gradients (sec. 5, ADR-0207). The first style on this list that is not a
# colour: every fill until now took an `rgba32` argument, and a gradient is an
# object with stops that has to outlive the call that sets it. Three symbols
# build one -- construct with the geometry, add each stop, release -- and two
# more on the context set it and put a colour back, because a fill style is
# context state and the next thing drawn did not ask to be a gradient.
#
# `bl_context_fill_path_d` is the plain fill, without the `_rgba32` suffix every
# other drawing call here carries: it fills with whatever style is set, which is
# the only way a gradient reaches a path.
#
# Shared work, and the reason it is worth the width: `goldberry-html` needs
# these for CSS gradients and `goldberry-vector` for SVG's, and both would
# otherwise start by adding the same five lines
# ([ADR-0190](../../../../../book/src/adr/0190-a-content-module-brings-its-own-natives.md)).
bl_gradient_init_as
bl_gradient_destroy
bl_gradient_add_stop_rgba32
bl_context_set_fill_style
bl_context_set_fill_style_rgba32
bl_context_fill_path_d

# Layers (sec. 5, ADR-0071). A subtree rendered into an offscreen image and
# composited back is what makes CSS group opacity mean what CSS means, and what
# lets an animating node re-blit a raster instead of repainting itself. These
# are the first two symbols added to this list since it caught its third
# local-symbol bug, and they are the only two the feature needs: the offscreen
# pixels are a direct ByteBuffer allocated in Java and wrapped with
# bl_image_init_as_from_data above, because Goldberry never asks Blend2D to
# allocate pixels.
#
# `img_area` was passed as NULL for a layer, which Blend2D reads as the whole
# image. A drawn image may want a part of one -- the crop an application carries
# on an image shape -- so a BLRectI crosses here now and `Layouts.BL_RECT_I` is
# the row that says what shape it is (ADR-0283). The C probe had always reported
# that struct; only the Java side was missing.
bl_context_blit_image_d
#
# And the scaled form, which is what a layer actually needs. A layer's raster is
# allocated in PHYSICAL pixels while the context it is composited onto is in
# LOGICAL ones, so blitting at an origin draws the raster one raster-pixel per
# logical unit -- correct at 1x by coincidence and twice the size at 2x, which is
# how every disabled control on a Mac came out enormous. The destination rect
# says how big the raster is in the context's own units, so the two spaces are
# reconciled once, here (ADR-0157).
bl_context_blit_scaled_image_d
bl_context_set_global_alpha

# Damage-driven painting (sec. 5, ADR-0072). Clipping the rasterizer to the
# region that changed is the half of damage tracking that saves the ~320us of
# rasterization rather than the upload. `restore_clipping` alone was enough while
# there was only ever one clip depth.
bl_context_clip_to_rect_d
bl_context_restore_clipping

# The second clip depth, which `canvas` is (sec. 1, ADR-0193). An application's
# own painter runs inside whatever clip and transform the tree already has, and
# `restore_clipping` goes back to the whole frame rather than to the previous
# region -- so a canvas inside a `scroll` would paint over the viewport's edge.
# A save/restore pair is the state stack the note above said was not needed, and
# it is needed exactly once a widget hands the context to somebody else.
bl_context_save
bl_context_restore

# --- Yoga (sec. 5, layout) ---------------------------------------------------
# The node API, bound in the `yoga` package. YGSize and the measure callback are
# above -- they were proven first (ADR-0017), and this is what finally drives
# them from a real layout pass.

# Config: the point scale factor is what makes fractional DPI land on physical
# pixels, and web defaults are what make Yoga agree with CSS. See ADR-0029.
YGConfigNew
YGConfigFree
YGConfigSetPointScaleFactor
YGConfigGetPointScaleFactor
YGConfigSetUseWebDefaults
YGConfigGetUseWebDefaults

# Node lifecycle and tree structure.
YGNodeNew
YGNodeNewWithConfig
YGNodeFree
YGNodeInsertChild
YGNodeRemoveChild
YGNodeRemoveAllChildren
YGNodeGetChildCount
YGNodeSetMeasureFunc
YGNodeHasMeasureFunc
YGNodeMarkDirty
YGNodeIsDirty
YGNodeGetHasNewLayout
YGNodeSetHasNewLayout
YGNodeCalculateLayout

# Style: the enum-valued properties.
YGNodeStyleSetDirection
YGNodeStyleSetFlexDirection
YGNodeStyleSetJustifyContent
YGNodeStyleSetAlignContent
YGNodeStyleSetAlignItems
YGNodeStyleSetAlignSelf
YGNodeStyleSetPositionType
YGNodeStyleSetFlexWrap
YGNodeStyleSetOverflow
YGNodeStyleSetDisplay

# Style: plain numbers.
YGNodeStyleSetFlexGrow
YGNodeStyleSetFlexShrink
YGNodeStyleSetAspectRatio
YGNodeStyleSetBorder

# Style: lengths. Yoga splits each property into points, percent and -- where
# the property admits it -- auto, which StyleLength puts back together.
YGNodeStyleSetWidth
YGNodeStyleSetWidthPercent
YGNodeStyleSetWidthAuto
YGNodeStyleSetHeight
YGNodeStyleSetHeightPercent
YGNodeStyleSetHeightAuto
YGNodeStyleSetMinWidth
YGNodeStyleSetMinWidthPercent
YGNodeStyleSetMinHeight
YGNodeStyleSetMinHeightPercent
YGNodeStyleSetMaxWidth
YGNodeStyleSetMaxWidthPercent
YGNodeStyleSetMaxHeight
YGNodeStyleSetMaxHeightPercent
YGNodeStyleSetFlexBasis
YGNodeStyleSetFlexBasisPercent
YGNodeStyleSetFlexBasisAuto
YGNodeStyleSetPosition
YGNodeStyleSetPositionPercent
YGNodeStyleSetMargin
YGNodeStyleSetMarginPercent
YGNodeStyleSetMarginAuto
YGNodeStyleSetPadding
YGNodeStyleSetPaddingPercent
YGNodeStyleSetGap
YGNodeStyleSetGapPercent

# The computed layout -- the whole point of the rest of it.
YGNodeLayoutGetLeft
YGNodeLayoutGetTop
YGNodeLayoutGetWidth
YGNodeLayoutGetHeight
YGNodeLayoutGetMargin
YGNodeLayoutGetBorder
YGNodeLayoutGetPadding
YGNodeLayoutGetDirection
YGNodeLayoutGetHadOverflow

# --- HarfBuzz (sec. 6, text) -------------------------------------------------
# Shaping: UTF-16 in (which is what a Java String already is), glyph ids and
# positions out. See ADR-0032.

# Which HarfBuzz is linked in.
goldberry_hb_version

# A font's bytes -> a face -> a font. Three objects because HarfBuzz separates
# the file, the typeface, and the sizing of it.
goldberry_hb_blob_create
goldberry_hb_blob_destroy
goldberry_hb_face_create
goldberry_hb_face_destroy
goldberry_hb_face_get_empty
# The units advances come back in when nothing sets a scale -- and therefore
# the units Blend2D's font matrix expects. See ADR-0034.
goldberry_hb_face_get_upem
goldberry_hb_font_create
goldberry_hb_font_destroy
goldberry_hb_font_set_scale

# The buffer: the run of text being shaped, and afterwards the glyphs.
goldberry_hb_buffer_create
goldberry_hb_buffer_destroy
goldberry_hb_buffer_reset
goldberry_hb_buffer_add_utf16
goldberry_hb_buffer_guess_segment_properties
goldberry_hb_buffer_set_direction
goldberry_hb_buffer_get_direction
goldberry_hb_buffer_set_script
goldberry_hb_buffer_set_language
goldberry_hb_buffer_get_length
goldberry_hb_buffer_get_glyph_infos
goldberry_hb_buffer_get_glyph_positions
goldberry_hb_script_from_string
goldberry_hb_language_from_string

# The one call the rest of it exists for.
goldberry_hb_shape


# --- Markdown (ADR-0294) -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Five symbols for a whole parser, and none of them is md4c's own. md_parse is a
# SAX parser: bound directly it would want five upcall stubs and would hand Java
# a detail struct per block, so what crosses instead is one encoded buffer per
# document -- parse it, read it, free it. The hot path does not cross FFM, which
# is the rule every content module shares (ADR-0190).
goldberry_md_parse
goldberry_md_data
goldberry_md_size
goldberry_md_free

# The HTML5 named-entity table, which ships inside md4c and is 2125 names. A
# second copy in Java would be a copy that drifts.
goldberry_md_entity


# --- WebP (sec. G35a, ADR-0329) ----------------------------------------------
#
# Three symbols, and no C glue: the rasterizer this toolkit ships is compiled
# with the PNG, JPEG and QOI codecs and no others, so a WebP -- which is what
# most screenshot tools now write -- did not decode at all. The format is VP8,
# which is a video codec, so there is no pure-Java answer worth writing.
#
# Bound directly rather than through a shim, which is sec. 3.1's rule: WebPGetInfo
# reads the size out of the header without decoding, WebPDecodeRGBA allocates and
# decodes one still frame, and WebPFree gives its buffer back.
#
# WebPFree and not free(): libwebp may be built against a different allocator
# than the one this process's free() uses, and upstream says so in as many words.
WebPGetInfo
WebPDecodeRGBA
WebPFree

# --- WebP, written and animated (ADR-0385) -----------------------------------
#
# The encoder. Two calls, because there are two answers and an application knows
# which it wants: a photograph is lossy at a quality, and a screenshot of a user
# interface is lossless -- flat colour and hard edges are what VP8's transform is
# worst at and what the lossless path is best at. Both allocate, both hand the
# buffer back through WebPFree above.
WebPEncodeRGBA
WebPEncodeLosslessRGBA

# The animation reader. `WebPAnimDecoderOptionsInit` and `WebPAnimDecoderNew` are
# static inlines in demux.h that forward to these with the ABI version, so the
# *Internal* names are what a binding can reach -- the version is passed from
# Java and checked by the library, which is exactly what the inline does.
#
# `WebPAnimDecoderGetNext` hands back a fully composited canvas and a timestamp,
# so the disposal model that animated GIF needed in Java (ADR-0382) is upstream's
# here. The buffer belongs to the decoder and is valid until the next call, which
# is why the binding copies before it asks again.
WebPAnimDecoderOptionsInitInternal
WebPAnimDecoderNewInternal
WebPAnimDecoderGetInfo
WebPAnimDecoderGetNext
WebPAnimDecoderHasMoreFrames
WebPAnimDecoderDelete
